 <office:body>
  <office:text>

   <!-- ===================== Letter 1: annual letter for 1971 ===================== -->
   <text:p text:style-name="Head">HARBOR LANE PARTNERS, LTD.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Head">41 HARBOR LANE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadLast">PORTLAND 4, MAINE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Date">January 21, 1972</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">How We Fared in 1971</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Every January I set down the year's results next to the "yardstick" we agreed on when each of you joined. It's a habit I intend to keep -- good years and poor ones alike -- because a record that is only reported when it flatters the reporter isn't much of a record.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">The Composite Industrial Average gained 9.3% during 1971 once dividends are counted. Our partnerships that operated for the whole year gained 21.7%, measured <text:span text:style-name="U">before</text:span> any allocation to the general partner. The full record since we began is below:</text:p>

   <table:table table:name="Results" table:style-name="T1">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T1.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T1.B"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T1.C"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T1.D"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Year</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Partnerships Operating Entire Year</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Partnership Gain</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Composite Gain*</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">2</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">14.2%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">6.1%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1969</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">3</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">9.8%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">-8.4%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1970</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">3</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">11.5%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">2.9%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1971</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">4</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">21.7%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">9.3%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">* Including dividends paid on the stocks in the Composite.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">After the general partner's share, and compounded from the start, the record for a limited partner reads:</text:p>

   <table:table table:name="Compounded" table:style-name="T2">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T2.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T2.B"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="T2.C"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Year</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Limited Partners' Gain</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">Composite Gain</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">11.6%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">6.1%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1969</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">8.1%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">-8.4%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1970</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">9.4%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">2.9%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1971</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">17.5%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">9.3%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder" table:number-columns-spanned="3"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"><text:span text:style-name="B">COMPOUNDED</text:span></text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:covered-table-cell/>
     <table:covered-table-cell/>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell"/></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">11.6%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">6.1%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968-69</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">20.6%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">-2.8%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968-70</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">32.0%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">0.0%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">1968-71</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">55.1%</text:p></table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="NoBorder"><text:p text:style-name="Cell">9.3%</text:p></table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">Keeping Score</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Last winter I wrote the following, and I would not change a word of it:</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Quote">"A single year tells you very little about a manager. Three years tell you a bit more, and a period that includes both a falling and a rising market tells you most of what you need to know. Please judge us on that longer stretch."</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">We now have such a stretch behind us, and the comparison has held up in both kinds of market. That is the only claim I care to make for it.</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">Where Our Money Goes</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Our commitments fall into three groups, each behaving differently from the others:</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Item">(1)<text:tab/>Undervalued common stocks bought for their own merits, which tend to move with the general market in the short run but should do better than it over a period of years.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Item">(2)<text:tab/>Special situations whose outcome depends on a corporate event -- a merger, a liquidation, a tender -- rather than on the level of the market. These earn a steadier, more modest return.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Item">(3)<text:tab/>Holdings large enough that we take a hand in the company's affairs. These are few, they take years, and they are the reason our results can look lumpy.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Early in the year a buyer for Rockport Canning was rumored, and by spring the terms were public: the figure was $4.15 per share.<text:line-break/>Mr. Abel attended the annual meeting in Boston, where the closing was put at sometime in the autumn.</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">Growing Pains</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Partners sometimes ask whether our growing size will make the job harder. The honest answer is that it will, eventually, but not yet. A small partnership can buy a few thousand shares of an obscure company without anyone noticing; a large one cannot. For the present our capital is small enough that the opportunities I find are limited by my own time rather than by our size, and I expect that to remain true for several more years.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">What size does change is the kind of company we can own outright. A few years ago a business worth a million dollars would have been far beyond us. Today it is within reach, and that opens a category of investment that was closed to us before. I would rather own a modest, honest business at a fair price than a glamorous one at a price that assumes everything goes right.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">I am often asked how I decide when a holding has run its course. There is no formula. When the price of a security reaches a level that fairly reflects its value, and something cheaper is available, we switch. When nothing cheaper is available we wait, and waiting has been one of our more profitable activities. I make no forecast of the market for 1972 and I do not know of anyone whose forecasts I would care to rely on. If the market should fall sharply, our comparative results should be good, although our absolute results may well be negative; if it should rise sharply, we will probably trail the Composite and you should not be surprised when we do. Over a full cycle, rising and falling, I expect us to do meaningfully better than the average, and I would be disappointed with any result that fell short of that over five years. Some of you have asked whether a good year should change any of these expectations. It should not. A good year mostly reflects the particular opportunities that happened to be available, and those come and go on their own schedule rather than on ours.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Our accountants have completed the audit, and each of you will receive a statement of your capital account with this letter. Tax information will follow by the middle of February.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Cordially,</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Edward T. Lindqvist</text:p>

   <!-- ===================== Letter 2: mid-year 1972 ===================== -->
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadFirst">HARBOR LANE PARTNERS, LTD.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Head">41 HARBOR LANE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadLast">PORTLAND 4, MAINE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Date">July 9<text:span text:style-name="Sup">th</text:span>, 1972</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">First Half Performance</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">During the first six months the market did very little, and so did we. The half-year figures were:</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Tabbed">Composite Industrial Average<text:tab/>-3.1%</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Tabbed">Harbor Lane Partners<text:tab/>4.6%</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="TabbedLast">Limited Partners<text:tab/>3.7%</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">We added to our position in the 6 1/2% notes of a regional railroad, which can be called at 104 1/4 and which we bought well below that level. The notes are not exciting, but they pay us to wait.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Cordially,</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Edward T. Lindqvist</text:p>

   <!-- ===================== Letter 3: special letter ===================== -->
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadFirst">HARBOR LANE PARTNERS, LTD.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Head">41 HARBOR LANE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadLast">PORTLAND 4, MAINE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Date">October 2, 1972</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">To My Partners:</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">A few practical points before year end:</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Item">(a)<text:tab/>Additions to your interest should reach us by December 20th so they can be credited from January 1st.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Item">(b)<text:tab/>Withdrawals follow the same calendar; please write rather than telephone so we have a record.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Sincerely,</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">Edward T. Lindqvist</text:p>

   <!-- ===================== Letter 4: annual letter for 1972 ===================== -->
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadFirst">HARBOR LANE PARTNERS, LTD.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Head">41 HARBOR LANE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="HeadLast">PORTLAND 4, MAINE</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Date">January 19, 1973</text:p>

   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">Our Results in 1972</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">The Composite rose 7.4% including dividends; we rose 12.9%. The largest contributor was Rockport Canning, whose figures appear in the appendix so you can follow the change in its balance sheet for yourselves.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Center"><text:span text:style-name="U">APPENDIX</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard"><text:span text:style-name="U">ROCKPORT CANNING COMPANY</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Standard">When we took control the company carried far more inventory than it could sell in a season. The comparison below shows what a year of patient housekeeping did:</text:p>

